{
  "id": "resolve",
  "name": "DaVinci Resolve",
  "kind": "app",
  "category": "Video",
  "match": {
    "windows_exe": [
      "Resolve.exe",
      "DaVinci Resolve.exe"
    ],
    "macos_bundle": [
      "com.blackmagic-design.DaVinciResolve",
      "com.blackmagicdesign.resolve"
    ],
    "window_title": []
  },
  "sources": [
    "https://documents.blackmagicdesign.com/UserManuals/DaVinci_Resolve_21_Reference_Manual.pdf",
    "https://www.blackmagicdesign.com/support/family/davinci-resolve-and-fusion"
  ],
  "note": "DaVinci Resolve 21. Blackmagic publishes no single consolidated shortcut list: the Keyboard Customization window inside the application is the reference, and the Reference Manual documents that window rather than its contents. These entries are therefore transcribed from the manual's own shortcut tables - the \"Keyboard Shortcuts in This Chapter\" tables of chapters 42, 43, 45, 46 and 51, the Edit page panel-focus table in chapter 40, and the Photo page shortcut tables in chapters 26/29 - plus a few shortcuts the manual states in prose (Shift-Z, Command-Plus/Minus, Shift-Command-A, Option-Command-K). Coverage is therefore strongest on the Edit page; the Color, Fusion, Fairlight and Deliver pages have no documented shortcut table in the manual and are not represented. The manual writes every shortcut in macOS notation and gives the conversion rule itself (\"About Keyboard Shortcuts\"): Option becomes Alt and Command becomes Ctrl on Windows and Linux, and the macOS Control key is unused by default; the Windows column here is that conversion. The manual's macOS \"Delete\" is the backspace key and \"Forward Delete\" the delete key, written here as Backspace and Delete. Left out because the chord grammar cannot express them: rows that are a bare modifier (Shift, Command, Command-Shift as drag modifiers) and the Media Pool arrow-key row that covers all four arrows at once. The transport bindings kept in `defaults` (arrow-key frame stepping, Space) are Resolve's long-standing defaults but are not in these tables.",
